-- `a <= b` as a decided proposition, and the crossings between it and `Nat/Le/Ind`.
--
-- The proposition itself is `/syn/Nat/Le`, because the compiler states window bounds in it and so must be able to spell it. The two forms are not interchangeable and neither reduces into the other: this one is decided, so a guard already taken discharges it for free but nothing can recurse over it; `Ind` is inductive, so a proof can walk its constructors but no comparison settles it. Everything here is the passage between them, which is what lets a bound be *established* by induction and then *stated* in the form a precondition wants.
--
-- The inductive form is a *child* of this module rather than its sibling, and the names say which is which: `Nat/Le` is the relation, `Nat/Le/Ind` is the relation done by induction. The pair used to be `Nat/Le` and `Nat/Le/Ind`, two spellings of one abbreviation that named the difference nowhere.
pub use /syn/Nat/{Le};
use /std/{Nat, Option, True, False};
pub mod Ind;
pub use Ind/{let Ind};

-- Immediate: `lte(n, n)` is decided by the shared inner alone, whatever `n` is.
pub let refl(n: Nat) -> Le(n, n) =
    True/qed();

-- Both arms land on something the comparison settles — `0 <= n` outright, and a shared successor by cancellation — so the recursion only walks the evidence it was handed.
pub rec of_ind(@a: Nat, @b: Nat, p: Ind(a, b)) -> Le(a, b) =
    match p: (x, y, w) => Le(x, y) | z(@_) => True/qed() | s(@_, @_, prev) => of_ind(prev) end;

-- The other direction, by cases rather than by walking evidence there is none of: a decided proposition has no constructors, so the recursion is on the numbers, and the case where the bound has run out is `False` under the hypothesis.
pub rec to_ind(a: Nat, b: Nat, p: Le(a, b)) -> Ind(a, b) =
    match a
    | 0 => Ind/z()
    | ap + 1 =>
        match b
        | 0 => match p end
        | bp + 1 => Ind/s(to_ind(ap, bp, p))
        end
    end;

-- Weakening on the right, for a bound that has to survive the index advancing past it.
pub let succ_r(a: Nat, b: Nat, p: Le(a, b)) -> Le(a, b + 1) =
    of_ind(Ind/succ_r(to_ind(a, b, p)));

-- The procedure that decides it, the `Le` twin of `Nat/Lt/try`. What a caller reaches for when it holds a position some other computation produced: the bound is a fact about that value, not about the code that made it, so it is settled by testing rather than by threading evidence back through the producer.
pub let try(a: Nat, b: Nat) -> Option(Le(a, b)) =
    match a <= b: (r) => Option(match r: (_) => Prop | true => True | false => False end)
    | true => Option/some(True/qed())
    | false => Option/none()
    end;

-- Transitivity, through the family that has constructors to recurse over.
pub let trans(@a: Nat, @b: Nat, @c: Nat, p: Le(a, b), q: Le(b, c)) -> Le(a, c) =
    of_ind(Ind/trans(to_ind(a, b, p), to_ind(b, c, q)));
